# Release Completion Rules

When a task authorizes commit, push, or deployment, completion means the
change is remotely available and, when deployment is authorized, verified
on the public site. A local commit is not completion.

## Before editing

1. Read AGENTS.md and relevant source-of-truth documents completely.
2. Run:
   - git status --short --branch
   - git remote -v
   - git fetch origin --prune
   - git rev-parse HEAD
   - git rev-parse origin/main
3. Preserve unrelated changes.
4. Use an isolated branch or worktree.
5. If pushing is requested, verify GitHub access before doing substantial work:
   git push --dry-run origin HEAD:refs/heads/cursor-access-check
6. If access fails, report it immediately. Do not wait until the work is finished.

## Recovering previous work

Never assume that a commit mentioned in conversation exists.

Verify it using:
- git cat-file -t <sha>
- git log --all --oneline --decorate
- git reflog --all
- git ls-remote origin

If the commit cannot be found, say that clearly and rebuild only from current
repository evidence. Never claim a missing commit was pushed.

## Implementation

1. Inspect the existing implementation and reuse existing components.
2. Make the smallest scoped change.
3. Do not invent routes, APIs, deployment services, or source-of-truth behavior.
4. Verify every internal link and external integration used by the change.
5. Preserve unrelated files and do not reformat large existing files unnecessarily.

## Required verification

Run the relevant:
- focused tests;
- lint;
- type checking;
- production build;
- git diff --check;
- final diff and status review.

Record exact commands and results. A skipped or unavailable test is not a pass.

## Commit and push

When authorized:

1. Stage only task files.
2. Create separately reviewable commits where appropriate.
3. Push the feature branch.
4. Verify the remote commit:
   git ls-remote origin refs/heads/<branch>
5. If production promotion is authorized, fetch origin again and confirm main
   has not advanced.
6. Never force-push.
7. Use the repository’s documented release path.

## Deployment

A successful push is not proof of deployment.

When deployment is authorized:

1. Identify the actual production service and whether auto-deploy is enabled.
2. Record the previous live revision.
3. Promote the reviewed commit through the documented path.
4. Monitor the deployment until it is live or failed.
5. Record the deployment ID and running commit.
6. Perform the requested public smoke checks.
7. If deployment fails, report the exact status and do not claim completion.

## Final report

Always report:

- branch;
- local commit SHA;
- remote commit SHA;
- whether push succeeded;
- deployment ID and running revision, if deployed;
- tests actually run;
- live URL checks;
- remaining blockers.

Never say “completed,” “published,” “pushed,” or “deployed” unless that exact
state was independently verified.

## Task prompt to paste next time

Add this short block to any Cursor task requiring a release:

This task is not complete at the local-commit stage.

Before implementation, verify that your current identity can push a feature
branch to this repository. If it cannot, stop immediately and report the exact
permission failure.

After implementation:
1. Run the required tests, lint, typecheck and production build.
2. Review the complete diff and git status.
3. Commit only scoped files.
4. Push the feature branch and verify the remote SHA.
5. If production deployment is authorized, re-fetch main, promote without
   force-pushing, monitor the real deployment to completion, and verify the
   public URL, sitemap, canonical, robots status and live revision.
6. Report exact commit and deployment IDs.

Do not claim success based on local files, a local build, an HTTP 200 fallback
page, or a commit that cannot be found remotely.
